When I stood up, I had covered three pages, though barely twenty minutes had passed. I walked into the communal kitchen and brought back an apple and some salad. I sat in a rocking chair, munching, and then hours later, after darkness fell, I walked out into a great tumble of stars. Although I was alone in my silent cell, I didn't feel alone. The people I loved felt closer to me than when they were in the same room. I took a long walk at daybreak along the monastery road, past benches here and there looking out to sea, and a smile from a stranger went through me as no sentence ever could.
当我站起来时，我已经写满了三页纸，尽管才过了不到二十分钟。我走进公共厨房，拿回一个苹果和一些沙拉。我坐在摇椅上，咀嚼着，几个小时后，天黑了，我走到外面，看到了一大片星空。虽然我独自一人在安静的牢房里，但我并不感到孤独。我爱的人感觉比他们和我同处一室时更亲近。我在黎明时分沿着修道院的路散步，经过这里和那里看向大海的长椅，一个陌生人的微笑穿透了我，这是任何句子都无法做到的。

101
00:54:26.000 --> 00:55:08.000
Often I just sat in a chair and did what is usually hardest for me, nothing at all. The monks who opened their doors, even to non-Christians like myself, made no demands on any visitor. They were ready to offer counsel if needed, but otherwise they were just working around the clock to ensure that all of us felt at home. When I stepped into the monastery bookstore on my second day, an elderly brother asked how I was doing. I love it, I said. He looked relieved, Clearly, silence wasn't always a blessing.
通常我只是坐在椅子上，做我通常最难做的事情，什么都不做。那些敞开大门，甚至对像我这样的非基督徒也敞开大门的僧侣们，对任何访客都没有要求。如果需要，他们随时准备提供建议，但除此之外，他们只是全天候工作，确保我们所有人都感到宾至如归。当我第二天走进修道院书店时，一位年长的修士问我过得怎么样。“我喜欢这里，”我说。他看起来如释重负，显然，沉默并不总是一种祝福。

102
00:55:08.000 --> 00:56:01.000
Of course, it was liberation to be away from every distraction. But mostly, I felt liberated from little Pico and all his chatter. I was freed of my social self and back in a silent self where I had no need of words or ideas. A lens cap had come off and now I could be filled by the world in all its wild immediacy. In the days that followed I simply read books or wrote letters to friends. I took the same walk again and again. Every morning when I awoke I had no designs upon the day. I let the moment decide whether I'd pick up a postcard or just look out to sea.
当然，远离一切干扰是一种解放。但最重要的是，我感到从“小皮科”和他所有的唠叨中解放了出来。我从社会自我中解脱出来，回到了一个沉默的自我，在那里我不需要言语或想法。镜头盖被取下了，现在我可以被世界以其所有的狂野直接性所填满。在接下来的日子里，我只是读书或给朋友写信。我一次又一次地走同样的路。每天早上醒来，我对这一天都没有任何设计。我让当下决定我是拿起明信片还是只是看向大海。

103
00:56:01.000 --> 00:57:00.000
Over the next few months and years I started going back. For two weeks, for three weeks, sometimes when the 15 retreat rooms were full, staying with the monks in their enclosure. Occasionally I went there when I was jet-lagged and even the silence couldn't help me then. Sometimes I arrived just as the radiance of the Big Sur coastline was shattered by torrential storms. All night I sat in my little trailer on the hill, unable to see another light or sign of human habitation. The wilderness felt merciless and terrifying. terrifying. But even when doubts or shadows arose, I realized I'd much rather confront them in this quiet sanctuary than when I was caught up in rush hour traffic or the cacophony of cable news.
在接下来的几个月和几年里，我开始回去。两周、三周，有时当15个静修室住满时，我就和僧侣们住在他们的围墙里。偶尔我有时差时也会去那里，那时即使是沉默也帮不了我。有时我到达时，大苏尔海岸线的光芒正被倾盆大雨所粉碎。整晚我坐在山上我的小拖车里，看不到另一盏灯或人类居住的迹象。荒野感觉冷酷而可怕。但即使当怀疑或阴影出现时，我也意识到我宁愿在这样一个安静的避难所里面对它们，而不是当我陷入高峰时段的交通或有线电视新闻的喧嚣中时。

104
00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:56.000
When my father was suddenly raced into the hospital, the only thing I could think to do was drive four hours one morning just to sit on a bench along the monastery road for two hours and then drive the four hours back. Isn't it selfish to leave your loved ones behind so you can go and restore yourself?" a kind friend asked me. Not if it's the only way I can learn to be a little less selfish, was my reply. When my daughter, at 13, was diagnosed with stage 3 cancer, I knew that sitting in silence above the blue-green waters for three days was the best way I could find the clarity and calm I would need as soon as I stepped back into her hospital ward.
当我父亲突然被送进医院时，我唯一能想到的就是一天早上开车四个小时，只是为了在修道院的路边长椅上坐两个小时，然后开车四个小时回来。“把你的亲人留在身后去恢复自己，这不是很自私吗？”一位好心的朋友问我。“如果这是我学会少一点自私的唯一方法，那就不是，”我的回答是。当我的女儿13岁被诊断出患有三期癌症时，我知道在蓝绿色的水域上方静坐三天是我能找到清晰和冷静的最好方法，这样我一回到她的病房就能用得上。

105
00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:55.000
I couldn't make Sachi's sickness go away, but I could try to protect her from my own useless worries and resentments. Spending time in silence put a frame around my agitated thoughts and disclose something real that stretched beyond and behind them. To come upon a place that exists outside the realm of constant change makes change a little bit less scary. In all my seven decades, I've never seen so many so close to despair as they are right now. A world is fractured, wars are breaking out on every side. Wildfires, like the one Wars are breaking out on every side. Wildfires, like the one that rewrote my life, tear through every hill. In those circumstances, the simple journey into
我无法让萨奇的病消失，但我可以试着保护她免受我无用的担忧和怨恨的影响。在沉默中度过时光为我焦虑的思想加上了一个框架，并揭示了一些超越它们并存在于它们背后的真实东西。来到一个存在于不断变化领域之外的地方，会让变化变得不那么可怕。在我七十年的生命中，我从未见过像现在这样有这么多人接近绝望。世界是破碎的，战争在四面八方爆发。野火，就像那场重写我生活的野火一样，撕裂了每一座山丘。在这些情况下，简单的旅程进入

106
00:58:55.000 --> 01:00:51.000
silence allows me to step out of the moment and into something more expansive. Not everyone I know can afford to go on retreat, but some liberation is always at hand if only you can sit quietly away from your devices, seek out a temple or church, just take a walk. Years ago, the Trappist monk Thomas Merton observed, When your mind is silent, then the forest suddenly becomes magnificently real. It's hard to get tired of the birdsong above that distant hill. So often it's my mind that makes my problems. It cuts the world up into you and me and complicates the simple. After more than a hundred trips into wide awake silence, I give thanks every time I come back to a reality far bigger than myself. Writer Pico Iyer. He has a new book about monasteries and the role they play in a secular age. It's called A Flame, Learning from silence. Hidden Brain is produced by Hidden Brain Media. Our audio production team includes Annie Murphy-Paul,
